%S A399695 1,1,4,1,3,10,1,3,7,20,1,3,6,13,35,1,3,6,11,22,56,1,3,6,10,18,34,84,1,
%T A399695 3,6,10,16,27,50,120,1,3,6,10,15,24,39,70,165,1,3,6,10,15,22,34,54,95,
%U A399695 220,1,3,6,10,15,21,31,46,72,125,286,1,3,6,10,15,21,29,42,61,94,161,364
%N A399695 Square array T(n,k), n >= 0, k >= 1, read by descending antidiagonals: T(n,k) is the number of nonnegative integer solutions to x + y + z + k*w = n.
%C A399695 T(n,k) is also the number of partitions of n into parts of size 1 (of three kinds) and size k (of one kind).
%C A399695 Also the degeneracy of energy level n of a 4-dimensional anisotropic harmonic oscillator with frequency ratios 1:1:1:k.
%C A399695 T(n,k) is the number of triples (x,y,z) of nonnegative integers with x + y + k*z <= n, i.e., the number of single-particle states up to level n in a 3-dimensional harmonic trap with frequency ratios 1:1:k (pancake-shaped for k > 1); equivalently, the number of particles in a spin-polarized, non-interacting Fermi gas filled to level n (O'Sullivan and Busch).
%C A399695 T(n,k) is the Wiener index of P_{n+2}^k, the k-th power of the path on n+2 vertices. For k <= n+2 it equals the sharp upper bound on the Wiener index of maximal k-degenerate graphs of order n+2 (Bickle and Che). For k > n, P_{n+2}^k is the complete graph K_{n+2}, whose Wiener index is binomial(n+2, 2).
%C A399695 Antidiagonal s = n + k has s terms. Its last term (k=1) is the tetrahedral number A000292(s) = binomial(s+2, 3). For s >= 2, its second-to-last term (k=2) is A002623(s-2). For k > n, row n becomes constant: T(n,k) = binomial(n+2, 2) = A000217(n+1); thus the first ceiling(s/2) terms of antidiagonal s are the initial triangular numbers.
%C A399695 Each column k is a quasi-polynomial in n of degree 3 with period k.
%C A399695 Column k=1 is A000292(n+1).
%C A399695 Column k=2 is A002623(n).
%C A399695 Column k=3 is A014125(n).
%C A399695 Column k=4 is A122046(n+3).
%C A399695 Column k=5 is A122047(n+2).
%C A399695 Column k=6 is A175724(n+4).
%C A399695 Column k=7 is A384328.
%C A399695 Column k=8 is A122056(n+2).
%C A399695 Row 0 is A000012.
%C A399695 The main diagonal T(n,n) is the central polygonal numbers A000124(n+1) = (n^2 + 3*n + 4)/2.
%H A399695 Jishnu Babu Ranitha, <a href="/A399695/b399695.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10010</a>
%H A399695 Allan Bickle and Zhongyuan Che, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00373-020-02264-8">Wiener indices of maximal k-degenerate graphs</a>, Graphs and Combinatorics 37 (2021) 581-589; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09202">arXiv:1908.09202</a> [math.CO], 2019.
%H A399695 Brian O'Sullivan and Thomas Busch, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.79.033602">Spontaneous emission in ultracold spin-polarized anisotropic Fermi seas</a>, Phys. Rev. A 79 (2009) 033602; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.0231">arXiv:0810.0231</a> [quant-ph], 2008.
%F A399695 T(n,k) = Sum_{m=0..floor(n/k)} binomial(n - k*m + 2, 2).
%F A399695 T(n,k) = binomial(n+2, 2) + T(n-k, k) for n >= k; T(n,k) = binomial(n+2, 2) for 0 <= n < k.
%F A399695 T(n,k) = Sum_{d=1..n+1} (n + 2 - d) * ceiling(d/k).
%F A399695 T(n,k) = Sum_{j=0..n} (n - j + 1) * (floor(j/k) + 1).
%F A399695 T(n,k) - 2*T(n-1,k) + T(n-2,k) = floor(n/k) + 1, with T(n,k) = 0 for n < 0.
%F A399695 T(n,k) = (M+1)*(k^2*M*(2*M+1) + 3*k*(2*r+3)*M + 6*(r+1)*(r+2))/12, where M = floor(n/k) and r = n mod k.
%F A399695 T(n,5) = floor((n+2)*(n+3)*(n+7)/30) (from Bickle's formula for A122047).
%F A399695 T(n,7) = floor((n+3)*(n^2+12*n+26)/42) (from A384328).
%F A399695 G.f. for column k: 1 / ((1 - x)^3 * (1 - x^k)).
%F A399695 For fixed k, T(n,k) ~ n^3 / (6*k) as n -> infinity.
%F A399695 T(n,1) = binomial(n+3, 3) = A000292(n+1).
%F A399695 T(n,n) = (n^2 + 3*n + 4)/2 = A000124(n+1).
%F A399695 T(n,2) - T(n-1,2) = floor((n+2)^2 / 4) = A002620(n+2) for n >= 1.
%e A399695 Square array T(n,k) begins:
%e A399695   n\k |   1    2    3    4    5    6    7
%e A399695   ----+----------------------------------
%e A399695    0  |   1    1    1    1    1    1    1
%e A399695    1  |   4    3    3    3    3    3    3
%e A399695    2  |  10    7    6    6    6    6    6
%e A399695    3  |  20   13   11   10   10   10   10
%e A399695    4  |  35   22   18   16   15   15   15
%e A399695    5  |  56   34   27   24   22   21   21
%e A399695    6  |  84   50   39   34   31   29   28
%e A399695    7  | 120   70   54   46   42   39   37
%e A399695 For example, T(6,4) = binomial(8,2) + T(2,4) = 28 + 6 = 34.
%t A399695 T[n_, k_] := Sum[Binomial[n - k*m + 2, 2], {m, 0, Floor[n/k]}];
%t A399695 Table[T[s - k, k], {s, 1, 12}, {k, s, 1, -1}] // Flatten
%o A399695 (Python)
%o A399695 from math import comb
%o A399695 def T(n, k): return sum(comb(n - k*m + 2, 2) for m in range(n//k + 1))
%o A399695 print([T(s - k, k) for s in range(1, 13) for k in range(s, 0, -1)])
%Y A399695 Cf. A000012 (row 0), A000124 (diagonal), A000217, A000292 (col 1), A002620, A002623 (col 2), A014125 (col 3), A122046 (col 4), A122047 (col 5), A122056 (col 8), A175724 (col 6), A384328 (col 7).
%Y A399695 Cf. A400322 (solutions for x + y + k*z + k*w = n).
